Partial only. I could not refute the "no full Russian translation" claim. The only Russian translation is the selection in «Памятники средневековой латинской литературы X–XII веков» (Moscow: Nauka, 1972). Ru.wikipedia gives pp. 449–459 for it.

Who translated it is disputed. Facetia.ru reprints the text (https://www.facetia.ru/node/2257) and credits it "Перевод М. Е. Грабарь-Пассек". Ru.wikipedia (article «Вальтер Шатильонский», raw wikitext) credits "Перевод М. Л. Гаспарова". The sweep took Gasparov from Wikipedia; facetia's explicit credit makes Грабарь-Пассек more likely. This needs checking against the printed volume. The archive.org scan (item 77012-1972) exists, but I could not read its text.

What the selection covers (facetia's section headings):
- the prose prologue;
- [Александр и Аристотель] I.27–115, 164–202;
- [Заговор Природы против Александра] X.1–30, 74–167;
- [Речь Александра к войску] X.299–329;
- [Смерть Александра] X.375–460.

That is about 370 lines of verse plus the prologue, roughly 7% of the poem. Nearly all of it falls in Books I and X: about a quarter of Book I and roughly half of Book X (my estimate).

Other Russian checks found no full or further partial translation:
- facetia.ru site search for «Шатильонский» and «Александреида» (the other hits are Walter's lyrics translated by Gasparov, plus Grabar-Passek's 1966 survey chapter, node/3654, which is not a translation);
- ru.wikipedia full-text search;
- ru.wikisource search;
- R. L. Shmarakov's bibliography (Alan of Lille, Bernard Silvestris, Walter Map, but no Walter of Châtillon);
- Crossref queries in Russian (nothing relevant);
- labirint.ru and Open Library searches for «Александреида» (nothing).

Search-engine access was blocked during this run (WebSearch budget used up; Yandex, DuckDuckGo and Rambler showed captchas), so the elibrary.ru and cyberleninka article space was not fully checked.

I found no full or partial Russian translation of any of the three Georgian versions. The only Russian renderings I could verify are short excerpts in A. S. Khakhanov, «Грузинская повесть об Александре Македонском и сербская Александрия». It was read to the Slavonic Commission of the Moscow Archaeological Society on 16.IV.1893 and printed in ЖМНП, ч. CCLXXXIX (1893, № 9), отд. 2, pp. 241–252. An 8-page offprint scan, licensed CC BY 2.0 AT, is at https://phaidrabg.bg.ac.rs/o:558 (I downloaded it and read every page). What it gives in Russian:
(1) the incipit of the prose text: «Считают царя Александра Македонского сыном Филиппа, но это ложь: ибо он сын царя египетского Нектанаба и супруги царя Филиппа Олимпиады»;
(2) the Georgian title in translation: «История Александра, царя Македонии и всего Востока, Севера и Юга, самодержавного царя царей»;
(3) a plot outline and scattered phrases, such as Alexander's fight with beast-men and the winged women on the iron mountain, and the founding of Edessa;
(4) a Russian verse translation of the 4-line prologue of Petre's poem («Этот рассказ на севере, в стране русских хранился… Перевел по-грузински Арчил…»).
Khakhanov's manuscript was an anonymous copy of 225 pages, late 18th or early 19th c., found in a princely library in Kakheti. He attributed it tentatively to Archil and derived it from a Serbian-redaction text, probably translated in Russia. The 1980 Georgian edition (see below) has a Russian résumé and the Russian title «Александрия», but that is a summary, not a translation.
Other checks: ru.wikipedia, facetia.ru's Alexander survey (https://facetia.ru/alexander-romances), which does not mention any Georgian version, and the ru.wikipedia biography of Khakhanov all came up negative. vostlit.info returned HTTP 504 and could not be checked. For Russian readers, Archil's source tradition, the Russian redaction of the Serbian Alexandria, is already available (Литпамятники 1965). A Russian version of Archil would therefore mostly be a back-translation.

overview:
The Islamicate Alexander tradition is much better served in English than in Russian. Russian has the great Persian and Chagatai poems: Firdawsi, Nizami, Navoi's «Стена Искандара» (БВЛ 1972) and, it turns out, Tarsusi's Dārābnāma (Kondyreva, Moscow 2000, cited in Venetis's review). It also has al-Biruni. The **Arabic** Alexander corpus is almost entirely absent in Russian. Vostlit's Tabari, Dinawari and Mas'udi translations are excerpts on the Caucasus, Central Asia and the Golden Horde, not on Iskandar. English now covers:
- the prose Iskandarnāma (Southgate 1978 abridged to about 1/5; Venetis 2017/18 complete);
- the Madrid Qiṣṣat Dhī l-Qarnayn (Zuwiyya 2001);
- Tabari's Ta'rikh (SUNY vol. 4);
- Tha'labi's Qiṣaṣ (Brinner 2002);
- the Epistola Arabica (Doufikar-Aerts).

The real gaps in both Russian and English are:
- Arabic wisdom and epistolary texts: the Rasāʾil (Maróth 2006 gives the Arabic text plus a study, no translation), Mubashshir's Akhbār al-Iskandar, Ḥunayn's Ādāb al-falāsifa;
- South-Arabian and exegetical Dhū l-Qarnayn material (Kitāb al-Tījān, Tabari's Tafsīr);
- chronicle chapters in Tha'alibi's Ghurar and Mas'udi;
- the Persian and Turkic poems after Nizami (Amir Khusrau, Jami, Ahmedi);
- Andalusi and Malay offshoots.

Several texts are not workable yet because there is no edition: the Sīrat al-Iskandar (Ṣūrī, 12 MSS, about 600 pp., unedited), ʿUmāra b. Zayd's Qiṣṣat al-Iskandar (only MS: BL Add. 5928), Nihāyat al-arab, and Manūchihr Khān Ḥakīm's Iskandarnāma. The Aljamiado Rrekontamiento could not be checked. Caveat: the web-search budget ran out halfway, so later status checks used catalogue and Wikipedia pages only. Russian "none" therefore often means "none found", and the Khusrau, Jami and Malay statuses need a library check.

