Best working text: R. Dankoff (ed.), Ahmedî, İskendernâme: İnceleme–Tenkitli Metin I–II (Ankara: TÜBA, 2020), free PDF at https://www.tuba.gov.tr/files/yayinlar/tibkm-serisi/978-605-2249-59-8.pdf (1,699 pp., 86 MB). It is free to read but "© TÜBA, tüm hakları saklıdır", so not openly licensed. Translating the 15th-century poem from it with citation is fine; republishing the edition's text and apparatus is not.
Free Latin-script control text: Akdoğan's Ministry e-book (archive.org/details/Skender-NNme-Ahmede). The archive.org item carries a Public Domain Mark, but the book itself says © T.C. Kültür ve Turizm Bakanlığı, so do not rely on that mark.
Public-domain witnesses:
- BnF Supplément turc 309 (Amasya 1416, the earliest MS), on Gallica, ark:/12148/btv1b8414994r;
- Walters W.664 (Aleppo 1486), check its licence;
- Ünver's 1983 facsimile of TY 921 (A);
- Gibb 1900 vols I and VI for the English extracts and their Turkish texts.
Section map with couplet numbers: Öztürk 2026, Appendix 1 (CC BY).

verification.ru_found:
The sweep's claim that no modern Russian translation exists is wrong. I found two modern Russian translations, and both include the Alexander chapters. Neither is made from the Syriac.

(1) From the GREEK. B. G. Derevensky translated Istrin's 1897 Greek text himself (Istrin, Texts pp. 5–50, after a Vatican MS). It appeared in «Учение об антихристе в древности и средневековье» (СПб.: Алетейя, 2000), roughly pp. 436–455. Kostuev 2023 («Теология: теория и практика») quotes it with page numbers from that edition, e.g. p. 449 and p. 451. It was reprinted as «Книга об антихристе» (СПб.: Амфора, 2007). The translation is headed «Слово Мефодия Патарского о царстве народов и последних временах, 5–12». It runs from the Alexander section to the end and leaves out the opening world chronicle (Adam to Gideon and the Ishmaelites). It covers Philip marrying Chuseth (Хусифа), daughter of Phol, king of Ethiopia; the unclean nations of the «Солнечная страна»; the mountains closing to 12 cubits; the bronze gates smeared with asyncite (ἀσύγκιτον); and the list of 22 nations. It continues with Byzas, Germanicus, Chuseth's marriage in Byzantium, the daughter Byzantia married to Romulus Armaleus, the sons Armaleus, Urbanus and Claudius, and the exegesis of Ps 67/68:32, then runs on to the end. It has notes and is online at https://facetia.ru/node/2860.

(2) From the SLAVONIC. L. N. Smolnikova translated the full Russian interpolated redaction from a 15th-c. Old Russian manuscript. It was published in «Апокрифы Древней Руси: Тексты и исследования» (сост. и коммент. В. В. Мильков; М.: Наука, 1997, 256 с.). This version includes the Alexander section: Philip marrying 'Olympia, daughter of Phol, king of Ethiopia'; the 'western/northern' mountains; the gates sealed with сунклит; and a list of Gog, Magog and the other kings. Table of contents: https://facetia.ru/node/5369. Text: https://facetia.ru/node/5380.

(3) Excerpt. The Povest' vremennykh let, s.a. 6604/1096, paraphrases Methodius on the nations Alexander shut in the mountains (the 12 cubits, the bronze gates, сунклит). The modern Russian translation of the PVL in БЛДР т. 1 includes it (http://lib.pushkinskijdom.ru/Default.aspx?tabid=4869). Note 498 there calls the passage a retelling of the «Откровение».

(4) Minor, not checked. Derevensky cites a Russian translation of Paul J. Alexander's article «Псевдо-Мефодий и Эфиопия» (Античная древность и средние века 10, Свердловск 1973). It probably quotes passages.

NOT FOUND: any Russian translation of the SYRIAC original. Reference works give none either:
- Словарь книжников entry (Дмитриев 1987);
- the ru.wikipedia «Издания» list, which mirrors the Православная энциклопедия article (Заболотный 2017, т. 45);
- Крюкова 2023;
- Кобищанов 2024.
These last three works and Kostuev's article still cite Istrin, or Derevensky for the Greek.

Method note: WebSearch was unavailable (session budget exhausted). In its place I used:
- about 12 Russian keyword searches through the CyberLeninka API;
- the facetia.ru site search;
- the Pushkin House e-library (БЛДР, Словарь книжников);
- the ru.wikipedia article and its bibliography.
